  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a light-driven water purification concept that uses tiny enzyme-coated magnetic microrobots inside a treatment chamber. Contaminated water enters a transparent box-like reactor, where light shines from above and magnetic coils are mounted around the sides. Inside the chamber, many small particles or robots are suspended in the water. A clean-water outlet on the opposite side suggests that the system removes pollutants as water passes through.\n\nThe enlarged schematic on the right shows the structure of one robot. It appears as a porous spherical adsorber with many surface-bound enzymes and an internal magnetic core or “engine.” Several radial, photoresponsive elements extend inward from the outer shell toward the magnetic center, implying that light can activate or modulate the robot’s behavior. The porous body likely provides a large surface area for capturing contaminants, while the enzymes contribute catalytic breakdown of pollutants. The magnetic component indicates the robots can be manipulated, collected, or stirred by the surrounding coils.\n\nOverall, the image presents a multifunctional purification system combining adsorption, enzymatic degradation, light responsiveness, and magnetic control to convert contaminated inlet water into cleaner outlet water.",
